\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Compatible compact-open reference subgroups}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite-dimensional rational vector space and let
#{Q} be nondegenerate. For each rational prime #{p}, put
#{V_p=\mathbb Q_p\otimes_{\mathbb Q}V} and let #{Q_p} be the extended
form. Write

##{\begin{gathered}
O_p=O(Q_p),\\
SO_p=SO(Q_p),\\
\Spin_p=\Spin(Q_p),\\
\Spin_p\xrightarrow{\rho_p}SO_p\xrightarrow{i_p}O_p.
\end{gathered}}

Here #{i_p} is inclusion and #{\rho_p} is the vector action. Spin is
the actual vector-generated even norm-one carrier of
\ref{fcap-0028}, with the canonical local topology of
\ref{fcap-0044}. The actions are continuous by \ref{fcap-0045}.
For a rational orthogonal or Spin point #{x}, write #{x_p} for its
scalar extension; the action compatibility is \ref{fcap-003N}.}

\p{A \newvocab{compatible compact-open reference tuple} for #{Q}
consists of subgroups

##{U_p^O\leq O_p,\qquad U_p^{\Spin}\leq\Spin_p}

at every prime, subject to the following requirements.}

\ol{
\li{Each subgroup is compact and open in its ambient local group.}
\li{The reference groups respect the vector action:
##{i_p\rho_p(U_p^{\Spin})\subseteq U_p^O.}}
\li{For each #{g\in O(Q)}, there is a finite set #{S_O(g)} of primes
such that #{g_p\in U_p^O} whenever #{p\notin S_O(g)}.}
\li{For each #{s\in\Spin(Q)}, there is a finite set #{S_{\Spin}(s)}
such that #{s_p\in U_p^{\Spin}} whenever #{p\notin S_{\Spin}(s)}.}
}

\p{The exceptional sets may depend on the rational point. The special
orthogonal reference subgroup is determined by these data:

##{U_p^{SO}=i_p^{-1}(U_p^O).}

Thus an element of #{SO_p} belongs to #{U_p^{SO}} exactly when its
underlying orthogonal transformation belongs to #{U_p^O}, and
#{\rho_p} restricts to #{U_p^{\Spin}\to U_p^{SO}}.}

\p{The subgroup #{U_p^{SO}} is open by continuity of #{i_p}. It is
compact because #{i_p} is the closed embedding cut out by
#{\det=1}, as in \ref{fcap-0045}: its image inside #{U_p^O} is closed
in that compact set. If #{g\in SO(Q)}, determinant commutes with
scalar extension, so the rational orthogonal membership condition gives
#{g_p\in U_p^{SO}} outside #{S_O(i(g))}, where
#{i:SO(Q)\to O(Q)} is inclusion. No further special orthogonal
family or exceptional-set axiom is needed.}

\p{For an affine finite-type scheme over a discrete valuation ring,
its integral points are open and closed in its fraction-field points,
as in \citet{Example 2.3, p. 3}{conrad2011adelic}. The paired orthogonal
and Spin data above additionally impose compatibility and rational
membership. TauCeti packages those exact fields in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens} and
defines the derived subgroup in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.specialOrthogonal}.
Its compactness is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.isCompact_specialOrthogonal}.
The formal structure allows arbitrary rational quadratic forms; the
nondegenerate setting here supplies the existence construction in
\ref{fcap-004D}.}
